    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3ddb21e9-c94b-5700-b789-7d0507e9b523",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sin\\left(52^\\circ\\right)\\tan\\left(26^\\circ\\right)-1}{\\sin\\left(26^\\circ\\right)+\\cos\\left(26^\\circ\\right)}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Write $s=\\sin\\left(26^\\circ\\right)$ and $c=\\cos\\left(26^\\circ\\right)$. The **double-angle formula** gives $\\sin\\left(52^\\circ\\right)=2sc$.\n\n Also, $\\tan\\left(26^\\circ\\right)=\\frac{s}{c}$.\n\nRewrite the quotient ratio and use $s^2+c^2=1$ to obtain a difference of squares. Factor before cancelling.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2sc\\frac{s}{c}-1}{s+c} &=\\frac{2s^2-1}{s+c} \\\\ &=\\frac{s^2-c^2}{s+c} \\\\ &=\\frac{(s-c)(s+c)}{s+c} \\\\ &=s-c \\\\ &=\\sin\\left(26^\\circ\\right)-\\cos\\left(26^\\circ\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll cancellations use nonzero factors in the original expression.",
